=== A. A stop becomes a market order; the stop is not a guaranteed fill ===
  sell stop 23; tape 24, 23.50, 22, 21.80
  triggered? True at print 22; market-order fill 22 (not the stop 23)
  the stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed execution price
=== B. A stop-limit may not be executed ===
  sell stop-limit stop 23 / limit 22.50; same tape
  triggered? True at print 22; limit fillable at or above 22.50? False
  a stop-limit becomes a limit order and may not be executed if the price moves away
=== C. Trailing-stop bulletin example: $20 -> $24 -> trigger $23 ===
  bought at 20; price peaks at 24; $1 sell trailing stop holds at 23
  as the price rises 20, 22, 24, the stop trails to 23
  price then drops 23.40, 22.90, 22.50
  triggered? True at print 22.90; fill 22.90 (may deviate from 23)
trigger convention (last-sale vs quotation) differs by firm; check with the broker
availability of stop, stop-limit and trailing-stop orders is firm-specific
assertions passed: 5/5
disclaimer: staff bulletin, not a Commission rule; learning only; no order-type recommendation
=== D. FINRA 5330: adjust an official open order on the ex-date, except less than one cent ($0.01) ===
5330(a): A member holding an open order from a customer or another broker-dealer shall,
  prior to executing or permitting the order to be executed, reduce, increase, or adjust
  the price and/or number of shares of such order by an amount equal to the dividend,
  payment, or distribution on the day that the security is quoted ex-dividend, ex-rights,
  ex-distribution, or ex-interest, except where a cash dividend or distribution is less
  than one cent ($0.01), as follows:
  (a)(1) Cash Dividends: Unless marked "Do Not Reduce," open order prices shall be first
  reduced by the dollar amount of the dividend
  (a)(2) Stock Dividends and Stock Splits: Unless marked "Do Not Increase," the size of
  the order shall be increased as officially provided
  official (a)(1)-(5) exist, including combined cash and stock and indeterminate value;
  this toy is not the rounding-formula catalog
5330(b): When a pending order involves a security that is the subject of a reverse split,
  the order (buy or sell) shall be cancelled
5330(c): When a pending order involves a security that is the subject of a stock split
  but is not otherwise required to be adjusted under this Rule, a member shall promptly
  notify the customer of the stock split
5330(d): The term "open order" means an order to buy or an open stop order to sell,
  including but not limited to "good 'til cancelled," "limit" or "stop limit" orders
  which remain in effect for a definite or indefinite period until executed, cancelled
  or expired
5330(e) exceptions include orders governed by the rules of a registered national
  securities exchange, open stop orders to buy, open sell orders, and orders where the
  issuer has not reported a dividend, payment, or distribution pursuant to SEA Rule 10b-17
  this toy invents that every open order is adjusted? False
  this is a rewrite of already-taught Rule 10b-17, or the rounding-formula catalog? False
  official open orders are adjusted on the ex-date, except where a cash dividend or distribution is less than one cent ($0.01)? True
no product, broker, or order-type recommendation
